WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.693
Universal life death benefit options.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:08.405
Option A versus Option B, and why the choice changes the cost.

3
00:00:09.334 --> 00:00:13.524
Option A, also called Option one, is a level death benefit.

4
00:00:13.618 --> 00:00:17.754
In this example, it stays at two hundred fifty thousand dollars for the life of the

5
00:00:17.774 --> 00:00:18.517
policy.

6
00:00:18.808 --> 00:00:22.603
As the cash value grows, it grows inside that face amount,

7
00:00:22.662 --> 00:00:26.365
so it's part of what the beneficiary receives, not extra.

8
00:00:27.329 --> 00:00:31.141
That means the insurer's actual risk, called the net amount at risk,

9
00:00:31.161 --> 00:00:32.437
shrinks over time.

10
00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:37.209
And less risk for the insurer means Option A costs less.

11
00:00:38.153 --> 00:00:42.065
Option B, also called Option two, is an increasing death benefit.

12
00:00:42.298 --> 00:00:45.699
The beneficiary receives the face amount plus the cash value.

13
00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:50.331
So the insurer's risk stays at the full two hundred fifty thousand dollars,

14
00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.677
no matter how much cash value builds up.

15
00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:58.087
Because the insurer carries more risk, Option B costs more than Option A.

16
00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:03.174
An easy way to remember it: A is level, and B is bigger.
